@Ecore(nsURI="https://skill.models.nasdanika.org", nsPrefix="org.nasdanika.models.skill")
@GenModel(
    modelDirectory="/model/src-gen",
    featureDelegation="Dynamic",
    complianceLevel="25",
    suppressGenModelAnnotations="false",
    copyrightFields="false",
    operationReflection="true",
    importOrganizing="true"
)
/*
 * DRAFT. Procedural knowledge that an agent loads when a task calls for it, and that a
 * person can read and follow.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MODEL
 *
 * The same test the tool model passed, passed more easily. A skill catalog has its own
 * audience (the people who know how a task is done), its own owners, and it is useful with
 * no language model in the picture at all, because a procedure written well enough for an
 * agent is a procedure a new colleague can follow. Tools needed that argument made; for
 * skills it is the plain case.
 *
 * Not part of the inference model, which is providers, models and sessions. A skill is not
 * tied to a provider, and moving between providers should not mean rewriting how things
 * are done.
 *
 * The agent model has a Skill class of its own, with a body and resources. This replaces
 * it, in the same way tool and example replaced the agent model's tools and examples, and
 * the agent model should get smaller by that much.
 *
 * ONE TEXT FOR TWO READERS, WITH ONE EXCEPTION
 *
 * The tool model keeps two texts apart: `description` is what a model is told,
 * documentation is what a person reads. For a tool they really are different texts. For a
 * skill they mostly are not: a procedure reads the same to a person as to an agent, and a
 * second copy written "for humans" is a copy that drifts. So the instructions are written
 * once, for both, and the documentation site renders them as they are.
 *
 * The exception is `description`. It is in context on every request, it decides whether
 * the skill is loaded at all, and it is paid for in tokens every time. That is routing
 * data with a budget, not prose, and it is kept apart for the same reason the tool model
 * keeps it apart.
 *
 * PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE, AS FEATURES
 *
 * A skill is disclosed in three levels: the description always, the instructions when the
 * skill is chosen, and resources when the instructions point at them. Each level is a
 * separate feature here, so what a skill costs in context at each level is a query over a
 * catalog rather than a guess. A catalog whose descriptions add up to more than a model's
 * budget for them is a finding, and it can be found before anything runs.
 *
 * The same layering is what makes the instructions readable by people: short, with the
 * depth in references.
 *
 * THE FOLDER IS THE SERIALIZATION
 *
 * The SKILL.md folder (a Markdown file with frontmatter, and scripts, references and
 * assets beside it) is how skills are already written and shared, and agents already know
 * how to load it. Competing with it would be pointless. This model is the CATALOG over it:
 * it loads existing folders into objects that can be inspected and reviewed, it generates
 * folders from skills authored here, and it adds what a folder of Markdown files cannot
 * say: which tools a skill expects from which catalog, which examples it draws on, where
 * it came from, and whether that has changed since anyone looked.
 *
 * Round trips are lossless for what the format carries. Frontmatter keys this model does
 * not know are kept as they are rather than dropped, because a format that evolves under
 * a lossy loader becomes a format that nobody here can update.
 *
 * Ownership, approval and review dates are not features here. They come from the role and
 * seal nano-models, as they do for every other catalog, and rendering them beside the
 * instructions is the first thing a documentation site adds over the folder.
 *
 * TRUST
 *
 * A skill is not documentation in the harmless sense. Its scripts are code, and its
 * instructions are text that steers an agent which holds tools, which is what prompt
 * injection is. A skill from outside is therefore a dependency, and it takes the same
 * route into an organization as one: received, pinned by digest, reviewed, then approved.
 * A skill whose content no longer matches its recorded digest is a different skill until
 * someone reviews it again.
 *
 * Skills are loaded and their scripts run at the trust level of the catalog they came
 * from. This is the rule for argument bindings in the tool model, one level up, and it is
 * equally cheap to state now and expensive to add later.
 *
 * WHERE A SKILL ENDS AND A TOOL'S INSTRUCTIONS BEGIN
 *
 * AbstractTool.instructions and ArgumentDiscovery are already small skills: how to use
 * this one tool, how to find valid values for that parameter. The line: guidance about
 * one tool stays on that tool, where everybody offered the tool gets it. Guidance that
 * spans tools, or is about a task rather than a call, is a skill.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS NOT
 *
 * Not a process model. The instructions are Markdown, and steps are not modeled. A skill
 * with steps worth modeling (ordered, assigned, tracked) has stopped being a skill and
 * become work, which has a model of its own.
 *
 * Not a persona. Who an agent is and how it speaks is the agent's system prompt, in the
 * agent model. A skill is what any agent that has it knows how to do.
 *
 * DEPENDENCIES
 *
 * On tool, because a skill names the tools it expects. The tool model knows nothing about
 * skills, and a tool is useful to an agent that has no skills at all.
 *
 * On example, for worked examples, which are often what makes a skill work at all.
 * Referenced rather than contained, as in the tool model.
 *
 * Depended on BY inference, whose Turn records which skills were offered and loaded, and
 * by the agent model, which says which agents have which skills.
 *
 * NOT on inference. Which model runs a skill is the caller's configuration, and a skill
 * that names a model has started to be about the model rather than the task.
 */
package org.nasdanika.models.skill

import org.nasdanika.models.example.ExampleCatalog
import org.nasdanika.models.example.ExampleSet
import org.nasdanika.models.nxcore.ModelElement
import org.nasdanika.models.nxcore.NamedElement
import org.nasdanika.models.tool.AbstractTool
import org.nasdanika.models.tool.ToolCatalog

annotation "http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore" as Ecore
annotation "http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/GenModel" as GenModel
annotation "urn:org.nasdanika" as Nasdanika

type Instant wraps java.time.Instant

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Catalog
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/*
 * A published set of skills. The unit of publication, of trust, and what a documentation
 * site is generated from.
 *
 * Catalogs compose by reference, as provider catalogs do: an organization publishes the
 * skills it has approved, a team points at that catalog and adds its own, and the
 * difference is a diff.
 */
class SkillCatalog extends NamedElement {
    contains Skill[] skills
    /*
     * Tool catalogs the skills here expect. Referenced, because the people who implement
     * tools and the people who write down how a task is done are rarely the same people.
     */
    refers ToolCatalog[] toolCatalogs
    refers ExampleCatalog[] exampleCatalogs
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Skills
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/*
 * A skill.
 *
 * `name` is human-facing; `skillId` is what an agent sees and what the folder is called.
 * The format constrains the identifier (lowercase, hyphens, short), and a title that
 * people read should not be bent to fit it. Same split, and same reason, as name and
 * modelId in the inference model.
 *
 * One class for authored and received skills. Unlike tools, where an authored tool has a
 * modeled signature and a received one has only schemas, a skill is Markdown whichever
 * way it arrived. What differs is provenance, and that is `origin`.
 */
class Skill extends NamedElement {

    /* The identifier in frontmatter and the folder name. */
    String skillId

    /*
     * Level one. What the skill does and when to use it, written for a model choosing
     * among many with a small budget. Always in context, so every word is paid for on
     * every request.
     */
    String description

    /*
     * Level two. The body: Markdown, loaded when the skill is chosen. Written once, for
     * people and agents both.
     */
    String instructions

    /* Level three. Loaded when the instructions point at them. */
    contains SkillResource[] resources

    /*
     * Tools the skill expects to have. Generates the format's allowed-tools field where a
     * target supports it.
     *
     * A reference into a tool catalog rather than a list of names, so a skill whose tool
     * has been retired or renamed is a dangling reference that a build reports, and not a
     * failure an agent discovers in the middle of a task.
     */
    refers AbstractTool[] tools

    refers ExampleSet[] exampleSets

    /* The format's license field: the terms the skill is shared under. */
    String license

    /*
     * The format's compatibility field: what the skill needs from the environment it
     * runs in, such as a product, packages or network access. Prose, as in the format.
     */
    String compatibility

    /* Frontmatter keys this model has no feature for, kept so that round trips are lossless. */
    contains MetadataEntry[] metadata

    /* Where a received skill came from. Unset for a skill authored here. */
    contains SkillOrigin origin
}

/*
 * A file the instructions refer to.
 *
 * Text content is inline, so a reference document is visible in a viewer, comparable
 * between versions and searchable with the rest of the catalog. Binary assets have a
 * location only, as attachments in the inference model have.
 *
 * Open question: whether a SCRIPT should be able to refer to an AbstractTool that
 * describes calling it, which would put it on the same invocation path as every other
 * tool. Left open until a skill needs it.
 */
class SkillResource extends NamedElement {
    SkillResourceKind kind
    /* Path within the skill folder, as the instructions refer to it. */
    String path
    String mediaType
    /* Inline text. Unset for binary assets. */
    String content
    /* Where the bytes are, for binary assets and for content that is not inline. */
    String location
}

enum SkillResourceKind {
    /* Documentation loaded into context when the instructions say to. */
    REFERENCE = 0
    /* Code that is run rather than read. Executable, and governed as such. */
    SCRIPT = 1
    /* Templates, images, data: used in output rather than read as instructions. */
    ASSET = 2
}

/*
 * A frontmatter entry with no feature of its own. Values stay as written, because
 * interpreting a key nobody here defined is guessing.
 */
class MetadataEntry extends ModelElement {
    String key
    String value
}

/*
 * Where a received skill came from, and what it was when it was received.
 *
 * The digest is the point. Skills are shared by copying folders, and a copied folder
 * changes without a release, a version or a notice. With a digest recorded at intake,
 * "which approved skills no longer match what was approved" is a query.
 */
class SkillOrigin extends ModelElement {
    /* A repository, a marketplace entry, a folder: wherever it was loaded from. */
    String uri
    /* A version, tag or commit, where the source has one. */
    String revision
    /* Digest of the skill's files at intake, algorithm included, e.g. sha-256:... */
    String digest
    Instant receivedAt
}
